On Sun, 30 Nov 2003, Dr. Richard E. Hawkins wrote:
>
[compiling old Nethack versions]
>
> In bothcases,I cp'd Makefile.unix to Makefile, then tried make.  Both
> bomb out on the same line in the makefile,
>
> char    inline[256], outline[256];
>
> (line 28 in  makedefs.c of 2.3)
>
> I'm using FreeBSD, but BSD is the default unix in those files (though
> it's changed a lot in the last many years.
>
> Any suggestions?

  The error here is the use of 'inline' as an identifier;
'inline' is a keyword in "GNU C" that's not in standard C.
Try adding some flags to gcc to disable that extension, and
see what happens:

  gcc -ansi <blah blah blah>

for example.  It may well be that '-ansi' is too strict; if
that's the case, then all I can say is to check the gcc web
pages for compiler options to turn off non-standard keywords.

HTH,
